drake parser increment 2, condition bindings derived, and the drake arrow (#53)

Four landings, in the order they happened.

1. PARSER INCREMENT 2 (#53) — rule and actor aspects, named arguments, call chains.

   Drake.parse now reads `rule` / `pattern` / `variables` / `conditions` / `action`
   and `actor` / `messageType` / `start` / `message` / `signal`. The two heads are
   read as FLAGS rather than as recursive sections: every sub-section carries a
   reserved head of its own, so the existing flat loop bounds them exactly as it
   already bounds domain / super / types. What the head contributes is presence — a
   rule with a wholly default pattern and action is still a rule.

   NAMED ARGUMENTS spell the marker BEFORE the name: `par = latitude cursor`.
   `par <name> = <value>` reads better and is ambiguous — `=` also opens a
   dyn-with-body's result line, so `… parameters par a` followed by `= r` parses
   equally well as the named argument `a = r` with the dyn's result missing, and no
   look-ahead settles it. `par` immediately followed by `=` occurs nowhere else,
   because an argument list that has run out of `par`s has already stopped. Same
   principle as the brackets on the opener: close the grammar rather than tie-break
   it. Transitional — the marker can move behind the name once the dyn `=` retires.

   CALL CHAINS fold back: a token opening with `.` is lexically a chain member and
   nothing else, since a path inside one expression (a.b.c, session.insert(…)) is
   glued into a single token by the lexer. A `.member` arriving after an argument is
   claimed by the PENDING CHAIN, not by the argument it just passed — both readings
   are legal and they differ only by indentation, which drake does not read. The cost
   is that an argument OF A CHAIN CALL cannot itself be a chain. Found by the focused
   round-trip test added for exactly these two forms, which the draco corpus does not
   exercise (both live in the mods actors).

   WHAT THE WIDENED GATE CAUGHT: the drake surface dropped factory.valueType
   entirely. Nine definitions carry `ActorType` rather than the enclosing type — the
   actor-minting convention, and what tells the Scala projection to mint an actor
   instead of an instance of the type. The `factory` keyword elides its value-type
   because it normally IS the enclosing type; when it is not, it is not
   reconstructable and has to be spelled. Draco.json had simply never reached a
   round-trip gate before, carrying an actor aspect.

   OPEN, and it keeps #44 from closing: a NESTED argument list has no terminator.
   An argument that is itself an application opens a `par` list of its own, and the
   enclosing list's next `par` is the only thing that could close it. Both readings
   are legal; domains/aerial/Input is the live case. Under BRACKETS this is an opener
   that does not yet bracket. Filed as #60; it is why the mods actors stay outside
   the parse gate.

2. CONDITION BINDINGS ARE DERIVED, NOT DECLARED.

   Dev's correction, and it dissolved most of what was built for it. The first
   attempt gave `con` a bracketed body — parameters block plus an `=` guard —
   borrowing the dyn-with-body shape. Wrong on three counts he named: the `=` read as
   an assignment of the parameter block; the parameters declared what the pattern
   already binds; and it conflated Condition with dyn-with-body, which are unrelated
   constructs. Reverted entirely. A `con` writes its guard and nothing else, as it
   always did.

   The invariant is ENFORCED, not conventional. `whereConditions` emits a Java string
   Evrete compiles at runtime, in which `$name` resolves only against the fact
   variables declared by `.forEach`; `conditionFunctions` emits `def w<n>` receiving
   exactly what `ctx.get[<valueType>]` yields. A declared parameter disagreeing with
   its variable fails either Evrete's runtime compile or scalac. There is no third
   possibility for it to be — so a stored copy could only ever agree, or be wrong.

   Expression.rootNames walks the tree for the identifiers that BEGIN a path (a `.`
   node contributes only its first operand's roots; `td.dracoAspect` mentions td, not
   dracoAspect). A string leaf is host-opaque, so there it falls back to a lexical
   scan — as far as anything can see into a value drake does not yet parse.
   Generator.conditionParameters filters the pattern's variables by it.

   Condition.parameters is gone: from the model (Condition.json's factory parameter
   and body element, and the generated apply / Null / decoder case), from
   Condition.drake, and from 7 conditions across 5 rule definitions. The generated
   Scala is byte-identical, which is the check that the derivation reproduces what
   was authored. Drake got SMALLER — with nothing to reconstruct, parse builds the
   Pattern directly.

   NAMED, NOT DONE: Condition.valueType ("Boolean"), Monadic's ("Unit") and Pattern's
